        <div id="terrain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Slopes, Soft Ground and Terrain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Slope is the factor that rules out the most machines. Every operator manual lists a maximum recommended slope, and residential riding mowers commonly fall somewhere around 10 to 15 degrees. That figure assumes dry grass, good tires and careful operation. Check the number for the exact model you are considering, then measure your steepest area with a smartphone inclinometer app before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lawn tractors handle moderate slopes more predictably than zero-turns because they steer with the front wheels. That does not make them safe on steep banks. On any slope, follow the manual's direction for mowing, which for most tractors is up and down rather than across, avoid sudden turns and starts, and never mow near drop-offs, ditches or water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soft or wet ground is a second issue. Heavy riders can rut lawns after rain, and zero-turns in particular can tear turf when turning sharply on damp ground. If your property stays soft for long periods, wider turf tires and a lighter machine help, and mowing only when the ground is firm saves the lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            For steep sections, use a walk-behind or a string trimmer. A rear-drive <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">self-propelled mower</a> handles banks safely where a rider should not go, and many large-property owners keep one alongside their rider for exactly that reason.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Safety first</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If you have to ask whether a slope is too steep to ride, mow it on foot.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rollovers on riding mowers cause serious injuries every year. Respect the manual's slope limit and treat it as a maximum for perfect conditions.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Choosing a Deck Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most entry riding mowers come with a 42-inch deck, and that size is a good fit for about one to two acres of grass with some obstacles. It fits through many garden gates, takes less storage space and maneuvers easily around trees. If your lawn is in that range and has a lot of beds and corners, there is little reason to go bigger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Decks in the 46- to 48-inch range suit roughly two acres, especially if a good portion is open. They cut more per pass but need a bit more room to turn and a wider gate. Many mid-range tractors and zero-turns offer this size as a step up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Decks of 50 to 54 inches and beyond pay off on large, open properties of two to three acres or more. They save meaningful time on long runs, but can be slower around many obstacles and may not fit through standard gates or into smaller sheds. Always measure gates and storage before choosing a wider deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck construction matters for longevity. Stamped decks are common on entry machines and fine if kept clean and dry. Fabricated or reinforced decks are heavier and more resistant to damage from roots and rocks. On rough properties, a sturdier deck is often a better investment than a few extra inches of width.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck leveling is worth learning early. A deck that sits lower on one side scalps the lawn and leaves an uneven, streaky finish. Most manuals give a simple procedure using the blade tips as reference points, and doing it at the start of each season is quick.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Common deck sizes and the acreage they suit</p>
