        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a zero-turn for sloped ground</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Zero-turns steer and brake through the rear wheels and can lose control on slopes and wet grass. If your property has noticeable slopes, a lawn tractor is more stable, and steep banks should be mowed on foot regardless of the machine. Measure your steepest spot before you shop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not measuring gates and storage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wider deck saves time on open lawns, but it may not fit through your gate, shed door or garage space. Take a tape to each gate, door and path the machine will use, including the discharge chute, before choosing a deck size. Remember the chute adds several inches to the overall width.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing gear drive for a busy lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gear-drive tractors require stopping to change speed, which is tiresome on lawns with many obstacles. If your yard has lots of trees, beds and turns, a hydrostatic tractor or zero-turn will be far more pleasant to use. Stopping to shift dozens of times per mow gets old quickly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping belt and blade maintenance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dull blades and worn belts cause most riding mower complaints. Sharpen blades several times a season, keep a spare belt on hand and check belt condition when you notice slipping, squealing or uneven cutting. Both are cheap compared with the downtime they prevent in the middle of mowing season when shops are busiest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Exceeding towing limits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Overloading a lawn tractor's hitch stresses the transmission and makes stopping on slopes dangerous. Check the manual's towing and tongue weight limits and reduce loads on inclines. Heavy loads on slopes push the tractor downhill and can overwhelm the brakes. Lighten the load and take a flatter route when you can.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

