        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which riding mower suits most one-to-two-acre lawns?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The John Deere S120 is a strong all-round choice thanks to its hydrostatic drive, V-twin engine and wide dealer network. If you want similar convenience for less money, the Cub Cadet XT1 LT42 is good value. For flat lawns full of trees and beds, the Toro TimeCutter zero-turn can save significant mowing time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Tractor or zero-turn: which is better for me?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a lawn tractor if your property has moderate slopes, if you want to tow carts or spreaders, or if you need snow attachments. Choose a zero-turn for mostly level lawns with many obstacles, where speed and maneuverability matter most. Steep areas should be mowed with a walk-behind regardless. Many owners of mixed properties pair a tractor with a trimmer for the steepest spots.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What deck width suits my acreage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For about one to two acres with obstacles, 42 inches is plenty. A 46- to 48-inch deck suits around two acres, and 50 to 54 inches or more suits larger, open properties. Always measure gates and storage space before choosing a wider deck, and consider obstacle density as well as total acreage. Owners who measure first rarely regret the size they chose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What slope can a riding mower safely handle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential riding mower manuals list a maximum somewhere around 10 to 15 degrees, but check the exact figure for your model. That limit assumes dry grass and careful operation. Mow slopes in the direction the manual specifies, avoid sharp turns, and use a walk-behind on anything steeper. No deadline justifies risking a rollover.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a battery riding mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your lawn is flat, within the mower's rated acreage per charge and you value quiet operation and minimal maintenance, a battery rider is worth considering. For larger or hilly properties, or if grass often gets long and wet, gas still offers more runtime and capability. Price the batteries you would need for your acreage before comparing totals with gas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What lifespan should I expect from a riding mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With regular maintenance, an entry lawn tractor commonly lasts around eight to ten years, and better-built tractors and zero-turns often last longer. Lifespan depends on how much you mow, slopes, towing, storage and how well you keep up with oil, blades and belts. Storage under cover and a seasonal service routine make the biggest difference to how long it lasts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is hydrostatic drive worth paying extra for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most buyers, yes. Hydrostatic transmissions give smooth pedal control without shifting, handle slopes and towing better than belt-driven automatics and make mowing around obstacles much easier. A gear-drive tractor is only a good fit for flat lawns with long, straight runs and a tight budget. Test both at a dealer if you can, since the difference in feel is obvious within a minute.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

